  "raw": "en Español\n\n423 \nABNT20 KNHC 311143\nTWOAT \n\nTropical Weather Outlook\nNWS National Hurricane Center Miami FL\n800 AM EDT Mon Aug 31 2026\n\nFor the North Atlantic...Caribbean Sea and the Gulf of America:\n\nNorthern Gulf of America (AL97):\nAn area of low pressure located a little more than 100 miles south \nof the coast of southern Louisiana continues to produce a small but \nconcentrated area of showers and thunderstorms with some signs of \norganization. Additional development is expected as the low moves \nslowly west-northwestward, and a short-lived tropical depression or \ntropical storm is likely to form before the system reaches the Upper \nTexas or southwestern Louisiana coasts on Tuesday.\n\nInterests along the southwestern Louisiana and Upper Texas coasts \nshould monitor the progress of this system, as tropical storm \nwatches or warnings could be required for portions of the area later \ntoday. Regardless of development, this system is expected to bring \nlocally heavy rainfall to portions of the Upper Texas and \nsouthwestern Louisiana coasts during the next couple of days. An Air \nForce Reserve reconnaissance aircraft is scheduled to investigate \nthe system this morning. Additional information on this system, \nincluding gale warnings, can be found in High Seas Forecasts issued \nby the National Weather Service.\n* Formation chance through 48 hours...high...70 percent.\n* Formation chance through 7 days...high...70 percent.\n\nNear Hispaniola (Remnants of Dolly):\nA surface trough of low pressure (the remnants of Tropical Storm \nDolly) is producing a disorganized area of showers and thunderstorms \nthat extends from near Hispaniola eastward to the northern Leeward \nIslands. Redevelopment, if any, would be slow to occur during the \nnext few days as the system encounters generally unfavorable \nupper-level winds while moving quickly west-northwestward at 20 to \n25 mph. Regardless, the system could bring locally heavy rains and \ngusty winds to portions of the Leeward Islands, the Virgin Islands, \nPuerto Rico, Hispaniola, and the Bahamas during the next couple of \ndays.\n* Formation chance through 48 hours...low...near 0 percent.\n* Formation chance through 7 days...low...10 percent.\n\n&&\nHigh Seas Forecasts issued by the National Weather Service can be \nfound under AWIPS header NFDHSFAT1, WMO header FZNT01 KWBC, and \nonline at ocean.weather.gov/shtml/NFDHSFAT1.php\n\n$$\nForecaster Reinhart"
